My Barbarians always screw it up.  While I happily make 
conduct-ascensions with other classes, I'm still stuck with 
my Barbarians.  Maybe it's bad karma because I abuse their 
cousins in other computer RPGs as cannon fodder and mage 
distractors.  Of course, they die mostly because of my fault.  
A classical example from yesterday:  

Gronsh managed to survive the protection dive (not a big 
surprise per se, but for my barbarians, it's not so trivial), 
and although he accidentally killed a coyote with a single hit 
immediately before entering the shrine, he could with tedious 
shoplifting gather enough zorkmids to afford AC 0 as a Lvl 3 
char.  All this effort, just to confuse #offer and #pray a 
thousand turns later and to lose the protection (of course, 
now being Lvl9).  Sigh.  All right, I wisely chose chaotic 
alignment, so we shall work on some heavy--duty brown pudding 
farming, get crowned (after getting 25 spellbooks[1], 38 golden glows, 
9321e12 shimmering lights, Trollsbane, Ogresmasher, Dragonbane, 
Leprechaunbane, Newtcrusher, GridBugslicer[2] and 2 protection points), 
go back to minetown, play with Stormy, and buy cheap lvl1 protection.  
Did the quest, almost trivial, got the lousiest artifact under 
earth (really, I never saw such a crap of an artifact), 
polypiled for some ?oEA and ?oEW, as well as /oteleport, polyied 
into a grey dragon and laid an egg, raised the dragon and slew 
it, blablabla, burned no less than 30 ?oteleport and 12 /oteleport 
just to get out of Gehennom with the amulet[3], blablabla, and 
then I am at astral plane, heading to the left first.  Ah, 
Pestilence, a wuss (slap slap with Stormy), now even wussier.  
Ah, a role character, I approach him..  
"$Whatshisname hits you with a long sword called Vorpal Blade.  
Vorpal Blade decapitates you!  You die.. "


Of course. 


It's so obvious.  


I know they can have artifact weapons, like Frosty and Fire Brand. 


It's only logical that they can have Vorpal Blade.  


The single potential Instadeath weapon besides the Tsurugi.


Hence, role monsters are to be considered extremely dangerous 
unless you can be sure they don't have Vorpal Blade; in fact, 
AFAICS, this is the only possible Instadeath threat on Astral 
Plane.  And one should never get near them blindfolded to 
initiate melee unless one has Vorpal Blade oneself.  


So it's a YASD.  Next time, I'll screw wishless and just wish 
for the damn thing myself, then bury it in a pit with a 
boulder on top, then burn Elbereth on the square and 
all adjacent ones, then build a boulder fort around it with 
a ?oEarth, then flood the level with brown puddings.  
[Or just over-enchant it, but that would be not as satisfying.]  
*This* won't happen again, I can learn a lesson.  


But why didn't this happen to a Tourist or a Healer or whatever 
character, but to a Barbarian again? 

Sigh.  


Next month, I'll try again.  


